I came to next to a patch of black ice  on a cold sidewalk somewhere in downtown  New Haven I was drunk I didn't know  where I was and felt completely lost the  only thing I need to do in that moment  was to sit there and start crying those  nights were all too common for me in my  early years of college the thing that I  most remember about that time was that I  just couldn't sit with myself I was  scared in addition to the normal angst  of being a teenager I was coming to  terms of the fact that I was attracted  to men I wanted things to be different I  wanted to be different and so I turned  to whatever I could to get away alcohol  drugs and my favorite and most  acceptable distraction work but no  matter what I did  inevitably I would come back to myself  just as I had that night in New Haven I  woke up the next morning with the  concerned face of a nurse hovering over  me in Yale New Haven Hospital when my  beam caught wind of the fact that I  drank myself into a hospital bed he  immediately summoned me into his office  I was terrified I thought that I'd blown  this once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to  go to Yale and that I was about to be in  a lot of trouble and yet when I walked  into his office that day he greeted me  with a big smile and a hug and he just  started asking me questions today I know  that he could sense the deep existential  angst that I was feeling at the time and  he recommended two books for me  one was a Christian inspired book called  traveling mercies the other was a  Buddhist inspired book called yoga and  the quest for the true self as I cared  those two books outside of his office  Tiffany he coyly said hey Vince you  might want to check out Church as well  life is hard sometimes we are find  ourselves in situations where we are  absolutely overwhelmed by the demands of  reality where we can't cope where the  only thing you want to do is crawl out  of our own skin or crawl underneath a  rock somewhere where do we turn when we  hit the black ice when we fall how do we  pick ourselves back up for many the  answer is religion but for me at the  time that would have been quite a leap  you see my religion during high school  was the anti religion I was a  card-carrying atheist and I loved  proving it to people around campus in  fact I remember one afternoon I got into  a fight with three Christian friends and  managed to bring all three of them to  tears as I poked cold holes of logic in  their Christian faith now I can't say  that I'm proud of the hurt that I caused  my peers that day but I can say that I  do understand some of the angst that I  felt towards religion at the time after  all I'd been brought up in a strict  Catholic upbringing and had experienced  some of the most hurtful aspects of  organized religion when I was in school  I would get physically whipped for not  being able to recite pieces of Scripture  or certain prayers when the first signs  of my attraction to men started to  bubble up I went under the worst type of  ridicule for my friends in school and a  much more subtle though no less hurtful  form of repression at home  all grounded in the deep religious  conviction that being gay was wrong and  that I was broken yes those tears that  afternoon in high school  those were my revenge against religious  zealot Ori and yet here I was pouring  again over Buddhist texts and Christian  scripture and actually starting to go to  church again you see the pain that I was  in at the time that feeling of  dislocation of alienation from myself  was even worse than the pain that  religion had inflicted upon me it felt  as if my whole world was just swirling  around me and I just needed something  steady to grab on to but if I was going  to go down that path again I was going  to do it on my terms  instead of adhering to any one type of  religious system or dogma I decided to  cast a wide net and began a spiritual  journey that took me from Christianity  to Zen Buddhism to Native American  animism and even the rationalism of  Enlightenment thinkers and over the  course of that journey I became a sort  of spiritual omnivore sampling different  pieces and ideas and concepts from these  religious philosophies in order to  construct my own worldview and my own  sense of meaning in the world over the  course of that journey there were three  habits that I noticed that were common  across each one of these philosophies  that helped me pick myself back up and I  hope will be helpful for you regardless  of where you are in your journey  the first is finding purpose in your  experiences seeking out the meaning and  opportunities that can arise from even  the most challenging circumstances in  traveling mercies the book recommended  by my dean she talks about herself as a  child of God  learning to walk on the earth and sea's  each relapse because she's a recovering  alcoholic as an opportunity for her to  get back up stronger than before now  while I couldn't quite subscribe to the  idea of God the Father and me as a child  I did like the idea of that the universe  would put these trials in my way as a  way of testing me  and making me stronger and so the  concept of a spiritual trial was one  that I added to my spiritual plate the  second idea or habit that I saw was  developing practices that help you honor  that purpose my first yoga instructor  was a woman named Maureen orange  straight out of the 60s  she was actually married to Michael  Bolton for a while rest in peace through  yoga Maureen taught me how to listen to  the body the language of my body and  emotions and taught me the power of  breath to keep myself grounded in  challenging situations instead of  running in the way that I had in the  past away from these types of stressors  today thanks to people like Maureen I  can hold myself in these moments and  share space even a time like today when  I'm pretty nervous as you can imagine so  that was a physical practice I added to  my plate as well  the final habit that I saw is that of  building community my friend Eliza and I  met in a Haitian dance class shaking our  booties which by the way is another  really fun spiritual practice and they  should try sometimes and since then  Eliza and I have continued to laugh  dance and cry together Eliza is the type  of spiritual body that I wish everybody  had when I fall she helps pick me up  when things are going alright she holds  me accountable and isn't afraid to call  me out on things  in an increasingly secular society with  all that's going on in the increasing  stress and chaos that we find ourselves  in it's more and more important for us  to know where to turn in those moments  of duress and yet as we have an  increasingly diverse and wide array of  spiritual philosophies the illness is  increasingly on us to make sense of our  own worldview and to make our own  meaning out of life over the course of  my journey I learned to put my faith in  three habits the question I leave with  you today is where you'll put your faith  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

